      "id": 21,
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## Method / known limits\n\nThis board is a **customer-shaped** port of an internal billable-actions design onto Temporal Cloud OpenMetrics. The internal original reads a ClickHouse warehouse (`chronicle.global_metric_billable_action_count_agg_30s / _agg_5m`) that no customer has. **None of the SQL ports. Only the decision design does.**\n\n### What survived the port, and what it became\n\n| Internal (ClickHouse) | Customer (OpenMetrics) |\n| --- | --- |\n| `sum(events_sum)` over a 30s/5m rollup table | `sum_over_time(temporal_cloud_v1_billable_action_count[w]) * 60` |\n| `account`, `namespace`, `workflow_type`, `action_type` | `temporal_account`, `temporal_namespace`, `temporal_workflow_type`, `action_type` - a near 1:1 label mapping |\n| `cluster` (data-plane cell) | `region` - coarser, but carries the same failover / migration signal |\n| `_unknown_` workflow-type data-quality panel | kept, on `$unattributed_re` |\n| separate 30s / 1m / 1h rollup rows | **dropped** - see below |\n\n### What did not survive, and why\n\n- **The three resolution rows are gone.** They existed because the warehouse holds a 30s rollup as well as a 5m one, so re-aggregating was free and the operator chose the grain. Cloud publishes **one 1-minute aggregate, full stop**: there is no sub-minute grain to expose and no coarser table to pick. Re-rendering the same series at 1m / 5m / 1h would be three views of one source dressed up as three fidelities. The time picker plus `$__interval` does that job honestly.\n- **Sub-minute burst detection is not recoverable.** A burst shorter than a minute is averaged into its minute at the source, before the customer ever sees it. Nothing in PromQL brings it back.\n- **`account` is a label here, not a dimension to pivot on.** A Metrics Read-Only key is account-scoped, so every series already carries one `temporal_account`. There is no cross-account picker because there is no cross-account data.\n\n### What the customer surface adds\n\n`temporal_cloud_v1_total_action_count` (with `namespace_mode` / `is_background`), `temporal_cloud_v1_action_limit`, and `temporal_cloud_v1_action_on_demand_envelope_limit` have no counterpart in the internal board. They turn a pure spend view into a **spend + throttling-risk** view, which is the more useful artifact in a production-readiness conversation.\n\n### The rate/counter trap\n\n`temporal_cloud_v1_billable_action_count` **is not a counter**, despite the `_count` suffix. It is a gauge carrying a per-second rate for a 1-minute aggregate, published with its own timestamp. Prometheus scrapes it at 30s with `honor_timestamps: true`, so the duplicate is dropped and the store holds **exactly one sample per minute, aligned to `:00`** (verified on this store). Consequences:\n\n- `rate()` / `increase()` produce garbage. The in-cluster scrape config says as much.\n- `avg_over_time(m[w]) * w_seconds` **overstates on a gapped series** - verified here: 7740 vs a true 258 over the same 30 minutes, because the mean of the published minutes was extrapolated across the idle ones. Totals on this board use `sum_over_time * 60` instead, which is exact and errs low across a scrape outage.\n- `avg_over_time` is still the right tool for the **projection** stat, where extrapolating the busy-minute mean is precisely the intent.\n\n### Unverified on this account\n\n- **The unattributed sentinel.** `$unattributed_re` defaults to `unknown|_unknown_|` (the trailing alternation matches the empty string). Which of those Cloud actually emits has **not** been observed here - this namespace has only ever produced `OrderWorkflow`-attributed actions. Confirm against a busy namespace before trusting the unattributed panels, and narrow the regex once you know.\n- **`temporal_cloud_v1_total_action_throttled_count` is absent** from this store, so no throttle panel is drawn. It is documented as a Cloud metric but appears only once a throttle has occurred; add the panel when you first see the series.\n- **`$stale_grace_min` defaults to 7**, inherited from the failover-readiness board where the ~209s publication lag on this same endpoint *was* measured. Not re-measured for this metric.\n\n### Reading it on an idle namespace\n\nCloud **omits** a billable-action series entirely when there is nothing to report, rather than publishing a zero. So on a quiet namespace the stats read `0` / `no samples` and Feed liveness reads `FEED SILENT / IDLE` - the board cannot distinguish \"no traffic\" from \"feed broken\" on this metric alone. Cross-check against the failover-readiness board's request-volume panels, which stay populated while workers are polling.\n"
      }
    }
